Sponsored Content – For many years, the Porous Transport Layer (PTL) has been regarded primarily as a component dedicated to fluid and gas transport within the electrolyser stack. With the growth of cell active areas and the transition from demonstration units to multi-MW and GW-scale industrial platforms, this perspective is rapidly evolving. Today, the PTL simultaneously influences several critical aspects of stack performance, including current distribution, contact resistance, compression uniformity, two-phase flow management, assembly repeatability, and overall industrial robustness. In other words, the PTL is evolving from a simple functional component into a true system-level design lever. Historically, porous materials have been evaluated mainly in terms of permeability and mass transport performance.

While these parameters remain essential, they are no longer sufficient to fully describe the behaviour of an industrial electrolyser stack. As active areas increase, mechanical stability, electrical contact uniformity, and the ability to maintain repeatable performance across large surfaces become increasingly important. Even small local variations can result in non-uniform current distribution, pressure imbalances, and inconsistent operating conditions. One of the key emerging topics in industrial electrolyser development is uniformity regarding thickness, compression and electrical and fluid-dynamic.

Recent research from the university of Tokyo by Kensei Tsuburaya further demonstrates that porous structures influence far more than fluid transport alone. Numerical simulations and experimental studies have shown that increasing electrode thickness does not necessarily lead to proportional performance gains. At industrial current densities, local current density distribution can become increasingly non-uniform across the thickness of the porous structure, causing only part of the available surface area to contribute effectively to the electrochemical reaction.

These findings underline the importance of designing porous architectures that simultaneously address transport phenomena, electrical conductivity and current distribution. These findings do not imply that thicker or multilayer structures are inherently disadvantageous. Rather, they emphasize the importance of engineering porous architectures in which transport properties, electrical pathways and mechanical stability are carefully balanced to ensure effective utilization of the active area. In practice, the objective is not simply to maximize porosity or surface area, but to create structures that enable uniform utilization of the active area while maintaining efficient gas removal and stable operating conditions.

Gas bubble management represents another important aspect of PTL design. Gas bubbles can locally block parts of the active surface, limiting ion access and reducing effective utilization of the available area. Engineered multilayer sintered structures offer the possibility to tailor porosity and flow paths through the thickness, supporting more efficient bubble removal. In addition, their porous architecture can be controlled with high uniformity across large surfaces, contributing to repeatable transport properties and stack performance.

Numerical simulations illustrate how porous electrode architecture influences local current distribution across the electrode thickness. At high current densities, only part ofthe available surface area may contribute effectively to the electrochemical reaction, highlighting the importance of engineered porous structures. Adapted from university ofTokyo by Kensei Tsuburaya. (2024)

Although PTLs are commonly associated with PEM electrolysis, similar requirements exist in AEM electrolysers. In the alkaline environment of AEM systems, nickel-based porous structures are typically used, making multilayer sintered architectures a promising solution for enhanced uniformity and stack performance.

The challenge is not simply achieving good initial performance, but ensuring that such performance can be reproduced consistently from stack to stack and from batch to batch. For this reason, concepts such as flatness, thickness uniformity, and repeatability are becoming increasingly central in discussions among OEMs, suppliers, and systemintegrators.

Stack performance does not depend solely on the individual components. It depends on the interaction between bipolar plates, PTLs, compliant layers, electrodes, and membranes. The quality of these interfaces can directly influence overall electrical resistance, pressure distribution, and the operational behaviour of the stack. As a result, the industry is progressively shifting its focus from optimizing individual materials to optimizing the entire interface system. Within this context, solutions based on multilayer sintered porous architectures are attracting growing attention. By combining metallic meshes with different geometrical characteristics, it is possible to engineer structures in which each layer contributes to a specific function, such as mechanical support, fluid distribution, two-phase flow management, porosity control, or dimensional stability. The sintering process creates a permanent metallurgical bond between the individual layers, transforming the assembly into a stable and repeatable monolithic structure.

Beyond their structural function, nickel-based multilayer porous architectures can also serve as a platform for catalytic surface modifications. Functional coatings such as NiFeOx can be integrated with engineered porous structures, combining controlled transport properties with enhanced electrochemical activity in AEM electrolyser systems. Meeting these requirements requires manufacturing technologies capable of controlling both microstructure and macroscopic dimensional accuracy across large areas.

For decades, BOPP has been active in the field of woven wire mesh and high-performance porous structures. The BOPP group, as specialist in diffusion bonding and multilayer sintering technologies, is capable of manufacturing engineered metallic architectures ranging from a few layers to highly engineered multilayer laminates. Today, this expertise is also being applied to the hydrogen sector, where multilayer sintered PTLs are being developed to address the requirements for uniformity, mechanical stability, repeatability, and scalability demanded by next-generation electrolyser technologies. The objective is to contribute to the development of engineered porous architectures tailored to the specific needs of each stack design.

As the hydrogen industry continues to evolve, the role of the PTL is becoming increasingly significant. Technical discussions are no longer focused solely on the material itself, but increasingly on topics such as uniformity, interfaces, compression behaviour, manufacturability, and industrial scalability. In this context, engineered multilayer sintered PTLs offer a promising approach to improving uniformity, repeatability and manufacturability in next-generation electrolyser platforms and their transition toward large-scale industrial deployment.

